{"subjectId":"java","id":"queue-deque","title":"Queue, Deque & PriorityQueue","group":"Collections Framework","order":46,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>Queue</code> = FIFO line, <code>Deque</code> = double-ended (queue <em>and</em> stack), <code>PriorityQueue</code> = always hands you the smallest element next. <code>ArrayDeque</code> and <code>PriorityQueue</code> cover almost every need.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-switch-horizontal\"></i>Two method families</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Action</th><th>Throws on failure</th><th>Returns special value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Insert</td><td><code>add(e)</code> — <code>IllegalStateException</code> if full</td><td><code>offer(e)</code> → <code>false</code></td></tr><tr><td>Remove head</td><td><code>remove()</code> — <code>NoSuchElementException</code> if empty</td><td><code>poll()</code> → <code>null</code></td></tr><tr><td>Look at head</td><td><code>element()</code> — <code>NoSuchElementException</code></td><td><code>peek()</code> → <code>null</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Prefer <code>offer</code>/<code>poll</code>/<code>peek</code> — empty checks become simple <code>null</code> tests. (Which is also why <code>ArrayDeque</code> and <code>PriorityQueue</code> refuse <code>null</code> elements.)</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-push\"></i><code>ArrayDeque</code> as a queue and as a stack</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Deque&lt;Integer&gt; queue = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();   // FIFO: offer at tail, poll at head\n        queue.offer(1);\n        queue.offer(2);\n        queue.offer(3);\n        System.out.println(queue.poll() + \" \" + queue.peek() + \" \" + queue);\n\n        Deque&lt;Integer&gt; stack = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();   // LIFO: push/pop at the head\n        stack.push(1);\n        stack.push(2);\n        stack.push(3);\n        System.out.println(stack.pop() + \" \" + stack.peek() + \" \" + stack);\n\n        Deque&lt;String&gt; dq = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();\n        dq.offerFirst(\"b\");\n        dq.offerFirst(\"a\");\n        dq.offerLast(\"c\");\n        System.out.println(dq.pollLast() + \" \" + dq);\n        System.out.println(new ArrayDeque&lt;Integer&gt;().poll());   // empty -&gt; null\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 2 [2, 3]\n3 2 [2, 1]\nc [a, b]\nnull</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Deque method</th><th>As a queue</th><th>As a stack</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>offerLast</code> / <code>offer</code></td><td>enqueue</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>pollFirst</code> / <code>poll</code></td><td>dequeue</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td><code>push</code> (= <code>addFirst</code>)</td><td>—</td><td>push</td></tr><tr><td><code>pop</code> (= <code>removeFirst</code>)</td><td>—</td><td>pop</td></tr><tr><td><code>peekFirst</code> / <code>peek</code></td><td>front</td><td>top</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Use <code>Deque&lt;Integer&gt; stack = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;()</code> — the Javadoc of <code>Stack</code> itself recommends it. <code>ArrayDeque</code> is a circular array: O(1) amortised at both ends, no node allocation.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sort-ascending\"></i><code>PriorityQueue</code> — a binary heap</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        PriorityQueue&lt;Integer&gt; min = new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;(List.of(5, 1, 8, 3, 2));\n        System.out.println(\"peek=\" + min.peek());\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        while (!min.isEmpty()) sb.append(min.poll()).append(' ');\n        System.out.println(\"min-heap order: \" + sb.toString().trim());\n\n        PriorityQueue&lt;Integer&gt; max = new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;(Comparator.reverseOrder());\n        max.addAll(List.of(5, 1, 8, 3, 2));\n        System.out.println(\"max first: \" + max.poll() + \" \" + max.poll());\n\n        PriorityQueue&lt;String&gt; byLen = new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;(\n            Comparator.comparingInt(String::length).thenComparing(Comparator.naturalOrder()));\n        byLen.addAll(List.of(\"kiwi\", \"fig\", \"banana\", \"date\"));\n        System.out.println(byLen.poll() + \" \" + byLen.poll() + \" \" + byLen.poll());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>peek=1\nmin-heap order: 1 2 3 5 8\nmax first: 8 5\nfig date kiwi</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>offer</code> / <code>add</code></td><td>O(log n) — sift up</td></tr><tr><td><code>poll</code> / <code>remove()</code></td><td>O(log n) — sift down</td></tr><tr><td><code>peek</code></td><td>O(1)</td></tr><tr><td><code>remove(Object)</code> / <code>contains</code></td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>Build from a collection</td><td>O(n) heapify</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Printing or iterating a <code>PriorityQueue</code> does <strong>not</strong> give sorted order — only the head is guaranteed to be the minimum. Poll repeatedly to get sorted output.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-trophy\"></i>Top-K with a size-K min-heap</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static List&lt;Integer&gt; topK(int[] nums, int k) {\n        PriorityQueue&lt;Integer&gt; heap = new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;();  // min-heap of the k largest so far\n        for (int x : nums) {\n            heap.offer(x);\n            if (heap.size() &gt; k) heap.poll();                 // drop the smallest\n        }\n        List&lt;Integer&gt; res = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;(heap);\n        res.sort(Comparator.reverseOrder());\n        return res;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(topK(new int[]{3, 1, 9, 7, 4, 8, 2}, 3));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[9, 8, 7]</code></pre></figure>\n<p>O(n log k) time, O(k) space — better than sorting everything (O(n log n)) when k ≪ n.</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why prefer ArrayDeque over Stack and LinkedList?</summary><div><p><code>Stack</code> extends the synchronised <code>Vector</code> (slow, and leaks list methods). <code>LinkedList</code> allocates a node per element and chases pointers. <code>ArrayDeque</code> is a resizable circular array: O(1) amortised at both ends, compact and cache-friendly. Its only restriction: no <code>null</code> elements.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you make a max-heap in Java?</summary><div><p><code>new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;(Comparator.reverseOrder())</code>, or <code>(a, b) -&gt; b - a</code> only when overflow is impossible — <code>Integer.compare(b, a)</code> is the safe lambda form.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Is PriorityQueue thread-safe? What about blocking queues?</summary><div><p>No. Use <code>PriorityBlockingQueue</code> for a thread-safe heap. For producer–consumer, the <code>BlockingQueue</code> implementations (<code>ArrayBlockingQueue</code>, <code>LinkedBlockingQueue</code>) add <code>put</code>/<code>take</code>, which block when full/empty.</p></div></details></section>"}